## Quick Reference

### Role of each component

| Component | Role |
|---|---|
| Arduino Uno | Reads sensors, runs filtering and state logic, drives outputs |
| Flame sensor | Detects IR radiation typical of flames (line of sight) |
| MQ-2 | Detects smoke/combustible gas as a conductivity change |
| Buzzer + NPN | Audible alarm, driven safely |
| Red LED | Warning/alarm visual |
| Green LED | Normal status |
| Amber LED / servo | Simulated suppression indicator |
| Push-button | Manual reset |
| LCD (optional) | Status display |
| Resistors | Limit LED and base currents |

### 60-second English interview explanation

"This is an Arduino-based educational fire detection prototype. It reads a flame sensor and an MQ-2 smoke sensor, filters the readings with a moving average, and debounces them to avoid false triggers. A single sensor produces a pre-alarm with a blinking red LED and short beeps. If both sensors agree, or one persists for a configured time, the system enters a latched alarm with a steady red LED and buzzer, and after a short delay it activates a simulated suppression output, an LED, for a fixed duration. The alarm can only be reset manually when the sensors are clear. The firmware also has a warm-up phase, serial diagnostics, and a basic fault state. I want to be clear that this is a learning project, not a certified fire alarm, and that thresholds need calibration and testing before drawing any conclusions."

### Potential industrial applications (conceptually, with certified equipment)

Server rooms and electrical panels (early-warning monitoring), small workshops and storage rooms, kitchens and ducts, and unattended equipment enclosures. A real deployment requires certified detectors, control panels, and professional design.

### Possible future enhancements

Temperature and CO sensors; EEPROM event logging; watchdog timer; wireless alerts (ESP32/GSM); real-time clock timestamps; supervised sensor loops; battery backup; PCB design; adaptive baseline tracking; and a calibrated test rig.
